# Tool sandbox

Register every domain tool in both `TOOLS` and `TOOL_FNS`. `SkillRegistry` slices those schemas by `allowed-tools` in each `SKILL.md`. A name in `allowed-tools` that is missing from `TOOLS` fails at import. Several skills may list the same tool. The loop sends `schemas()` for the loaded skills plus `load_skill` and `unload_skill`, not the full `TOOLS` list, and sends each tool name once. `execute_tool` stays the raw sandbox; `SkillRegistry.execute` rejects a domain tool whose skill is not loaded and names the skills that list it.

A tool's own contract (limits, paging and how to continue, match rules, refusals) goes in its schema description, not in a `SKILL.md` body. `TOOLS` descriptions do not depend on the session. `tools/scope.py` maps a tool to its `SCOPE` kinds (`read`, `path`, `process`, `shell`, `net`); `schemas()` sends a copy whose description ends with `scope_clause` for the registry's `yolo` and `project`, so the sandbox rules (blocked shell syntax, confined paths, public hosts) or their yolo forms travel with the tool into any skill. MCP tools are not registered in `TOOLS`. Once their skill is loaded, `execute` sends the call to that server's stdio session. The server command comes from `mcp` in the YAML config. The child is not passed through Seatbelt. A tool result is MCP text content passed through `cap_result`, or `Error: ...` when the server reports `isError` or the process has died. The next call after a dead process starts a new one.

Resolve every file path with `resolve_in_workspace` (`Path.resolve` plus `is_relative_to`). Reject empty paths and anything outside the workspace. When `execute_tool` is given a project, relative paths use that directory and absolute paths may resolve under the project or the workspace. Downloads still resolve inside the workspace only.

Return failures as `Error: ...` strings. `execute_tool` catches `ValueError` and `OSError`. `_tool_result` turns bad JSON into a tool error and still returns `(args_or_none, result)`. Integer arguments go through `_positive_int` / `_count_arg`, which reject zero, negatives, and booleans with `Error: <name> must be a positive integer`.

Cap model-facing text with `cap_result` (`MAX_TOOL_RESULT`). Every truncation marker must tell the model how to continue: pass a `hint` (a saved text file plus `read_file` with `offset`, the next page, a narrower pattern).

File tools: `read_file` pages with 1-based `offset` and `limit`, cuts a long result on a line boundary, and ends with the next `offset`. `read_file`, `delete_file`, `edit_file`, and `move_file` refuse directories. `write_file` and `append_file` create parents and report the byte count. `write_file` and `edit_file` then `compile()` a `.py` file and append `Syntax error:` without refusing the write. `edit_file` requires `old_text` to match exactly once unless `replace_all`. `move_file` never overwrites. `list_files` and `search_files` skip `SKIP_DIRS`, cap output at `LIST_FILES_MAX` / `SEARCH_FILES_MAX`, and skip files that are not UTF-8 text.

`run_shell` stays `shell=False`, allowlisted env, `timeout` default `SHELL_TIMEOUT` capped at `SHELL_MAX_TIMEOUT`, optional `stdin`. Block `SHELL_META` and `DENIED_COMMANDS` (`rm`, `mv`, `chmod`, `sudo`). Reject `..` in argv parts. Check absolute args with `resolve_in_workspace`. When the formatted stdout and stderr exceed `MAX_TOOL_RESULT`, the full text is saved under the workspace `.shell/` directory and the truncation marker names that absolute path and the next `read_file` offset. `_run_process` drains the pipes on threads; once stdout and stderr together pass `SHELL_MAX_OUTPUT` bytes the process group is killed, later chunks are dropped, and the result carries a `note:` line before `stdout:` saying so. `run_python` runs `sys.executable -I -c code` through the same `_run_process` with the same limits; it has the same trust level as `run_shell`. `run_script` runs `sys.executable -I <path>` plus a list of string `args` through `_run_process`. It rejects a missing file, a directory, a non-`.py` path, and a non-string argument.

Both commands go through `sandboxed_argv` in `sandbox.py`. On macOS the child calls `sandbox_init` before exec. The Seatbelt profile allows read and write only in the workspace, plus the project when one is granted, and read and execute of the OS and Python runtime roots. `HOME` and `TMPDIR` (`workspace/.tmp`, skipped by `SKIP_DIRS`) point at the workspace. The working directory is the project when one is granted, otherwise the workspace. Network is denied. If the sandbox cannot be applied, including on any OS other than macOS, the command is not run.

`--yolo` sets a context var for the `execute_tool` call. Read tools (`read_file`, `list_files`, `search_files`, `parse_html`, `read_pdf`, `read_docx`, `inspect_csv`, `inspect_json`) then allow paths outside the workspace. `run_shell` skips `prepare_shell` and runs `/bin/sh -c` with the parent environment, so pipes, redirects, chaining, and `$` work. `_run_process` does not call `sandboxed_argv`. `SHELL_META` still blocks those forms outside yolo. `write_file`, `edit_file`, `append_file`, `delete_file`, `move_file`, and `run_script` still resolve inside the workspace, or inside the project when one is granted. HTTP tools skip the public-host check and may use localhost and private addresses. `http` and `https` only, with no userinfo, still apply.

HTTP: `validate_url` allows only `http` and `https`, with no userinfo. Resolve the host and reject any address that is not global, and do the same on every redirect. The openers use `_PinnedHTTPHandler` and `_PinnedHTTPSHandler`, whose connections resolve and vet the host again when the socket is opened and connect to one of those addresses, so a DNS answer that changes after validation cannot reach a private address. In a yolo session `_assert_public_host` returns immediately, so localhost and private addresses are allowed for every HTTP tool, including redirects and `web_search` result URLs. Disable proxies. `fetch_url`, `read_url`, and `http_get_json` go through `_open_request` with a browser-like user agent; bodies are capped at `FETCH_MAX_BYTES`, requests at `FETCH_TIMEOUT`, and media types must be in `FETCH_TYPES` (HTML, plain text, Markdown, CSV, JSON, XML and feeds, PDF). `fetch_url` saves the body in the workspace (default `downloads/<host>-<hash><ext>`, extension from the media type) and returns status, content type, byte count, and path. `http_request` uses the same URL, proxy, timeout, and redirect checks, but returns status, headers, and body for any status code. `json_body` is an object or array (a JSON string of either is accepted) and is sent as `application/json`. `body` is raw text. Redirects stay off unless `follow_redirects` is true. The body is read up to `FETCH_MAX_BYTES` (`truncated: true` when the rest is dropped) and the text result goes through `cap_result`.

`parse_html` reads a saved workspace file and returns title, author, date, and text through `extract_page`: trafilatura article mode, then trafilatura with `favor_recall`, then a stdlib text fallback; `mode: full` goes straight to the fallback. It does not download. `read_url` is fetch plus extract in one call: it saves the raw file and a `.txt` copy, and its truncation hint names that text file. PDFs go through pypdf, stop after `PDF_MAX_PAGES` pages, and end with a marker naming the saved file and the `read_pdf` `first_page` to continue; other text types are returned as-is.

`web_search` POSTs the query to DuckDuckGo's HTML results page with optional `site:` and `df=` (`time` in `d`, `w`, `m`, `y`). Parse titles, snippets, and `uddg` redirect targets with the stdlib HTML parser. Drop a result whose URL is not public http(s). Default to 5 results and cap at `SEARCH_MAX_RESULTS`. Empty queries are `Error: query is required`. A page with nothing parseable is `Error: no results`. The result ends with `SEARCH_HINT` so the model reads pages instead of answering from snippets.

`read_feed`, `read_docx`, and any other XML go through `_parse_xml`, which refuses `DOCTYPE` and `ENTITY` declarations before calling ElementTree. `read_docx` checks the `word/document.xml` member size against `FETCH_MAX_BYTES` before inflating it. `get_json` accepts only `JSON_TYPES`; `http_get_json`, `wikipedia_search`, and `wikipedia_summary` build on it.

`remember` / `recall` keep `## key` sections in `BASE_DIR/MEMORY_FILE` (`~/.harnh3x/memory.md` by default), shared across sessions and outside the workspace. `memory_keys` reads that file for the system prompt. `write_plan` / `update_plan` / `show_plan` keep `N. [ ]` lines in `PLAN_FILE` inside the workspace.

`system_info` lives in `tools/system/`: `__init__` holds the schema, the section filter, and the platform dispatch, `probe.py` the `HostProbe` that runs commands and reads files, `format.py` the shared parsers and row formatters, and `darwin.py` / `linux.py` the per-platform reports. It reads the host outside the seatbelt. It runs a fixed set of read-only commands and files for OS, machine, CPU, memory, storage, and attached hardware. It does not take a command string. On Windows and any other platform it returns `Error: system_info supports Linux and macOS only`. A failed probe is a note in the report.

```python
# BAD: shell=True, or raising into the loop
subprocess.run(command, shell=True, cwd=workspace)

# GOOD: argv from prepare_shell, errors as strings
return f"Error: {exc}"
```
